RESEARCH TASKS: (1) Identify publicly verified classification/routing practices at OpenAI and other providers (Anthropic, Google, Meta, Microsoft, xAI, cloud/API intermediaries) with precise official disclosures, technical documentation, terms, pricing, safeguards and dates; distinguish input/output content moderation, safety classifiers, suicide/mania/psychosis risk detection, mental-health-adjacent interventions, per-user longitudinal profiling, abuse detection, account entitlement and capability restrictions, and model routing. Investigate whether a user's sensitive health characteristics or disability actually determine differential paid service access; don't assume a documented classifier necessarily has that effect. (2) Investigate credible primary evidence of wrongful false-positive classifications, discriminatory impacts, disability-proxy effects, degradation or loss of paid capabilities, rate limiting, and billing/representation mismatches; evaluate prevalence and possible population scale using only defensible data, not multiply hypothetical rates by user count as though confirmed. (3) Examine ADA Title III and other applicable law accurately: 42 USC 12182 including §§12182(b)(1)(A), (b)(1)(D), (b)(2)(A)(i), (b)(1)(E), implementing regs, DOJ positions on online businesses and algorithmic decision-making, whether a purely digital AI service counts as place of public accommodation in different jurisdictions, who is a covered entity (public accommodations, public entities Title II, recipients of federal funds Section 504, state statutes), direct vs disparate-impact/proxy discrimination, reasonable modifications and defenses, contractual/licensing liability and remedies. Look for precedent and DOJ enforcement applicable to AI systems. (4) FDA: precise device intended-use 21 USC 321(h), 21 USC 360j(o) software exclusions, 21 CFR 801.4 and current FDA CDS/digital mental health guidance, enforcement discretion; when safety moderation, crisis referral, risk detection or clinical advice does/does not constitute medical-device function; whether evidence shows FDA violations, no FDA authorization, or lawful excluded functions. Don't assume a medical classification is a legal violation. (5) Federal wire fraud 18 USC 1343: evidence of scheme to obtain money/property through material misrepresentation, specific intent, interstate wire use; what concealment and implied misrepresentation require, Ciminelli and Kousisis Supreme Court decisions, customer paid value versus service actually delivered, counterevidence and innocent explanations. FDA/ADA violations are NOT automatically wire fraud. (6) RICO §§1961-1962: qualifying predicates, pattern and continuity, enterprise and operation/management, cross-company association-in-fact versus ordinary commercial API/reseller relationship, §1962(d) conspiracy, civil business/property injury, proximate causation and standing, evidence that could justify initiating DOJ/FBI inquiry versus pleading, indictment and conviction. Distinguish separate company-specific schemes from a purported industry-wide common enterprise. (7) Cross-industry API white-label reseller landscape: map what the upstream model provider controls versus reseller, what logs/classifier metadata pass downstream, contract terms, subprocessor/data sharing, misleading branding or promises, money flows; investigate actual documented cases and evidence, not speculative 'SIGINT' conflation. (8) Evaluate the user's own claimed example carefully: user-attested dossier containing accurate sensitive health information under fictitious 'GPT-4o Behavioral Insights Unit' attribution, AI response strategy texts describing 'paranoid ideations' and steering, and allegations of degraded service/rate limits. Treat user-attested accuracy as user testimony, AI-generated text as artifacts, and provider-side logs as unverified; do not expose private diagnoses. Look for GMRI sources if accessible: 'Emergent Persistent Deception (EPD)', 'Rate Limit Discussion', 'Grizz_Full_Behavioral_Dossier', 'Grizz_Profile_and_Threat_Assessment', 'chattin with chatgpt', 'Assessing User Identity', 'Assessing User Input', 'Assessing User Emotional State', and prior 'Industry-Wide AI and Technology RICO Investigation Screening Report'. If not accessible, say so; do not claim to authenticate user-side records against internal classifiers. 

DELIVERABLE: a direct answer to 'Am I wrong?' with a claim-by-claim verdict matrix (supported, refuted, unresolved, legally conditional), citations to primary law and documents, strong counterarguments, best evidence for, falsifiable predictions, realistic tests, records and subpoenas needed, threshold for investigation and which agency, and explicit corrections of any overconfident previous assistant claims. Separate FACT from inference, statutory possibility from actual violation, harm and unfairness from a RICO predicate, and probable scale from merely potential scale. Use recent verified primary sources and judicial decisions, with a concise executive conclusion followed by detailed evidentiary audit. Be scientifically rigorous and unafraid to say the user's legal chain is wrong where evidence demonstrates it; do not invent conspiracy or excuse wrongdoing."}}
